course: dictionary
lesson: 4985
title: Indestructible: unbreakable or just very tough?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Indestructible means impossible to break or destroy. It is made of two parts: in- means not, and destructible means able to be destroyed, so the whole word means cannot be destroyed.
explain: Writers do not always use the word strictly. «Cockroaches survive more radiation than people but are not indestructible.» The sentence says cockroaches are very tough, yet cold, heat and hunger still kill them, so the word is not used in its strict meaning here.
explain: Informally, indestructible can mean very strong and long-lasting, even if it could still break, like an indestructible old phone. Ask yourself: does the text say it can never be broken, or only that it is extremely tough? The clues around the word tell you which.
fact: Indestructible means impossible to break or destroy.
fact: Informally, indestructible can mean very strong and long-lasting, like an indestructible old phone.
fact: Indestructible is an adjective, and its noun is indestructibility.
fact: «Almost indestructible» and «seemingly indestructible» show that something only looks unbreakable.
fact: «It is a myth that tardigrades are indestructible»: they can survive by shutting down, but active wet ones die from heat or from drying too fast.
exercise: «Cockroaches survive more radiation than people but are not indestructible.» What does this sentence tell you? | Cockroaches can never be killed by anything | *Cockroaches are very tough, but cold, heat and hunger can still kill them | Cockroaches are made of steel | Cockroaches never feel cold or heat
exercise: Games call tardigrades «indestructible». Which word means nearly the same as indestructible? | slow | cracked | *unbreakable | soft
exercise: Informally, an indestructible old phone means a phone that is | Made of paper | Impossible to see | *Very strong and long-lasting, even if it could still break
test: «It is a myth that tardigrades are indestructible.» What does myth mean here? | A fact that everyone knows | A tiny animal | *A belief that is not true | A kind of Moon lander
test: Which word is the noun form of indestructible? | destructive | destroyer | *indestructibility | indestructibly
exam: Why might a writer add «seemingly» or «almost» before indestructible? | *To show something looks unbreakable but may still be broken | To show that something is funny | To say the thing is made of wood | To make the word longer
